# Flow API design

General Facts:

Fearless has two kinds of errors: deterministic and non deterministic (like stack overflow). `Try#` catches deterministic errors anywhere; observing a non deterministic one needs `mut CapTry` from `mut System`.
In the current standard library Repr is mostly complete, while IsoPod is still under design and may end up offering most of the functionality of Repr, allowing reads and mutation of the IsoPod without opening it.

## Properties to be respected by any part of the language

**Determinism:**\
A method with no mut parameters (including the receiver) returns structurally identical results for structurally identical input. "Results" includes deterministic exceptions and termination: the result may be non termination or a structurally identical thrown Info.

This works mostly because every method of an object capability doing something non deterministic is a mut method: with no mut in input, any capability in the ROG of the operation is 'muted'. Most of the properties below also support determinism, but are valuable on their own.


**Immutability:**\
An imm reference refers to an object whose ROG is deeply unmodifiable: nothing in that ROG will ever change again.

**read:**\
A read reference can not be used to mutate an object or anything in its ROG.
If a method has no mut parameters, any read parameter is also deeply unmodifiable for the duration of the call, since no mut reference to its ROG can be in scope. This allows us to reason inductively about deterministic parallelism: if a read binding `r` is in scope, there will be no other active parallel execution with a mut reference to any part of `r`'s ROG.

**iso:**\
An iso reference refers to an object whose (mutable) ROG is encapsulated under that single reference. The ROG may share imm objects, which are referentially transparent, but every mutable object in it would be garbage collectable if that iso reference were lost.

**Strong exception safety:**\
The body of a try either completes successfully or leaves the state observable outside of the try unmodified. Enforced by typing: `Try#[R](try: read F[R])`. A `read` lambda cannot mutate what it captures, so the body reaches only what it created or the `iso` given to the two-argument form, and `Action` yields the value or the `Info`.

Example: with `rs` of type `mut List[mut Repr[T]]`,
`Try#{.. rs.flow(ParRepr).map{r -> r.mutate{...}}.list}`
compiles only if `rs` is created inside the `{..` part, not captured by the `Try` lambda.
Thus running that flow as a fork-join is correct: even if element 3 of 7 of `rs` throws, no non determinism is observable, since all of ROG(rs) is garbage collectable when the error is captured.


**Identity Transparency:**\
Without the mut methods of capability objects, we can not distinguish whether two references are the same object or just structurally equivalent.\
This is enforced by careful API design in all the `_Magic!` setups, and is needed for Determinism and caching: otherwise one could call something twice and tell re-computation from a cached result, violating Determinism of an outer expression that internally uses caching (and tests for identity).

**Flow unobservable parallelism:**\
It must not be observable whether a potentially parallel flow actually runs in parallel or sequentially. This is intentionally slightly stronger than what Determinism alone needs.

Where a method contract is purposely under-specified, such as the tournament order in max, min and sort, the same choice must be made in every mode: a different winner under parallel and sequential execution would make the mode observable.

This property enables a crucial optimisation: a terminal can check at its start whether all the cores are busy (`allTheCoresBusy`) and, if so, run that flow sequentially.


**Flow abstraction:**\
Observably, the flow must behave as if it trickled the elements through the stages one at a time.
The exception is a fence operation: it emits nothing downstream until every stage before it has finished with every element, and if its lambda can mutate what it captured (only .forEach) it does not run that lambda until then either. A fence operation may still work as elements arrive - a sort may place each one as it is received - provided nothing escapes before the stages before it have settled, a thrown Info included.

It must not be observable whether a flow runs the stages one at a time or passes the elements one at a time through all the stages, whether it does internal materialisations, etc.


**Flows overall design:**\
A flow expression is a source, then stages, then one terminal.

**Lazy:** No user provided lambda of any flow method runs until the terminal is triggered.

**Semantics:**\
The semantics is given by multiple descriptions, validated by checking that they all agree:
1. the simple composition of all the stages and the terminal;
2. desugaring all flow operations into more primitive ones, until a few operations remain with an un-optimised but trivially correct direct implementation;
3. examples of many thorny cases;
4. translation of full flow expressions into traditional imperative statements.

The terminal operation picks the execution strategy by looking at all the stages in the chain; this enables optimisations, like skipping unneeded internal materialisations.


**Stages** are the intermediate operations returning another flow.

**Elementwise:** `map`, `filter`, `flatMap`, `flatMapMut`. The output for an element depends only on that element, so any element can run on any thread.

**Streaming stateful:** `limit`, `scan`, `actor`, `actorMut`, `map with context`, `distinct`. They need the elements in order and emit as they go.

**Fence:** `sort`, `sortDistinct`, `max`, `min`. They consume everything before emitting anything.

**Source combinator:** `++`. A fence that then appends the elements of a list to those of the flow, rather than acting on elements.

**Terminals** end the flow and return something that is not a flow.

**Consuming:** `list`, `eList`, `set`, `eSet`, `mapping`, `forEach`, `fold`, `foldAcc`, `size`, `get`, `getOpt`, `join`, `sum`, `last`, `let`. Every element passes the whole chain.

**Short-circuit:** `first`, `any`, `all`, `none`, `findFirst`, `findMap`. They can decide before the end, leaving later elements unreached.

**Execution modes:**\
The static type of the source collection enables one or more execution modes. They crystallise what we know and can assume, and allow or block execution strategies for the terminal, including parallelisation and omission of intermediate materialisations.

**`Par[T:imm,read]`:** can be enabled if source elements are `imm` or `read`.

**`ParRepr[T]`:** can be enabled if source elements are `mut Repr[T]`. `Reprs#` takes an `iso T`, so two distinct `Repr`s share no inner state. This does not ensure the `Repr`s in the source are distinct objects: the flow runs an identity check over the source and raises a non deterministic error when two elements are the same object.

**`ParPod[T]`:** can be enabled if source elements are `mut IsoPod[T]`, with the same guarantee through `IsoPods#` and the same identity check.

The modes are not about the type of the elements between the stages. A stage of a flow in execution mode `Par` may create mutable objects, and another stage may mutate them: over an `imm List[imm Nat]`, `l.flow.map{i -> Persons#i}.map{p -> ...}` runs in parallel and every (mut) `Person` it builds is reachable from nowhere else, so later stages can mutate it safely.

**Duplicated `mut` elements:**\
`flatMapMut` can emit one `mut` object as two elements, and so can `actorMut`, by pushing its element twice:
```
numbers.flow.map{n -> Persons#n}.flatMapMut{p -> Lists#(p, p)}.filter{..}.list
```
Each `Person` now appears twice, so two tasks could run `filter` on the same `Person` at the same time: the lambda of `filter` is a `read F`, which can not mutate what it captured but can mutate its `mut` parameter. Two elements can also share a `Person` without being it, like two pairs holding the same `Person`, so no identity check can find this.
So the note above stops holding after a `flatMapMut` or an `actorMut`: the elements they produce from the same input may share `mut` objects, and can not be handed to different tasks. Any strategy that hands the elements after them to different tasks, like splitting again after a fence or running the stages after an `actorMut` in parallel, has to respect this.

**`onlyImmCapture` flag:**\
The backend marks each lambda object that captures only `imm` values; the flag is visible to the runtime (in Java), not in Fearless.
`ParRepr` and `ParPod` need it on every lambda of the flow except those of `forEach` and `let`, which run once every stage has finished; without it the flow raises a non deterministic error (see below).
The identity check only compares the elements with each other, while a `read` lambda can capture any `mut` binding as `read`: the source list itself, or a local or parameter `p: mut Repr[T]` that is also one of the elements. Such a lambda can read that `Repr` while the task of its element mutates it, and the parallel execution becomes observable.
No `imm` value can reach a `mut Repr` or `mut IsoPod`, so with the flag each task reaches no `Repr` or `IsoPod` but its own element.
