`.seqFlow` exists only on sources whose elements may be `mut`, such as `List` and `EList`, to flow them as `mut` without a mode. Where the elements are always `read` or `imm`, as in `Set` and `ESet`, `.flow` is the only entry point: running in parallel or sequentially is not observable there, so a preference for sequential execution is only a hint (see **Chunk sizes**).

Calling `.flow` with calls suggesting that parallelism is possible, while passing parameters or calling methods that prevent any parallelism, is most likely a user mistake. When the type system can not check this statically, a non deterministic dynamic error is raised. A linter could catch more cases statically, but never all of them, since flows under construction are first class values that can travel freely through the code.

A good example of why a flow of Repr could be great is genetic evolution:
a list of large Repr elements can be //TODO: make this example more concrete.
- fitness evaluated in parallel with a map, fitness result stored in the mut Repr object
- sorted by fitness
