Note how actors are not used in this example.

**Speculative execution:**\
There are two kinds: positive and negative.

**Positive speculative execution** is mostly about short-circuit operations: check 8 elements in parallel against a predicate, then return the first one that satisfies it. This is problematic for example with Reprs: we could mutate elements that sequential execution would not touch, exposing a visible side effect and breaking the properties.

**Negative speculative execution** is about deterministic exceptions: even something as 'obviously parallelisable' as `list.flow.map f .list` is non trivial to parallelise correctly when a deterministic exception may interrupt the mapping in sequential execution. Strong exception safety may mitigate this, but the sections below must carefully consider the impact of both kinds.

## The two kinds of parallelism

**Data parallel/fork-join**

This is the iconic map reduce set up, where
`a.flow.map f1 .filter f2 .list`
can divide the flow into chunks (parallel tasks), give some chunks to workers and leave some 'for later'.
Each worker then conceptually applies
`f1#e |> {x->f2#x .thenDo{acc.append x}}`
with some accumulator strategy `acc` that preserves the intended position of each element.
This is often the favourite parallelisation strategy.

**Pipeline parallelism/Nodes**

This is what happens in actor systems, where
`a.flow.map f1 .filter f2 .list`
is conceptually made of 3 actors/nodes.
The first receives (chunks? individual?) elements from the flow, applies f1 and sends them to the mailbox of the next, which applies filter f2 and sends the survivors to the last one, building the list. Crucially the 3 actors run in parallel and can freely update local mutable data: each sees a sequential view of its task.

This strategy applies in plenty of cases where the other does not, and it is more efficient when code locality is crucial: the same actor does the same operation over and over, and if that code fits in cache this may beat data parallelism, especially when the full operation (not broken into chunks) would not fit in cache.

## Per operation

The kinds above say what an operation is. Each section below is the design space of one operation: what an implementation is free to do with it, and what is still open.

### map, filter, flatMap

These operations act element-wise. Their lambda is a `read F[E,R]`, so it cannot mutate anything it captured; the only possible mutable input is the element it is handed, which may be `mut`.

**Semantic reduction:**\
map and filter are both trivially implemented on top of flatMapMut.

`.map f -> this.flatMapMut{e->Lists#(f#e)}`

`.filter f -> this.flatMapMut{e->f#e?{.then-> Lists#e; .else->{}} }`

They exist as separate methods for readability and performance.
map goes 1-1, so it preserves the size, and returning exactly 1 element avoids the 'sequence API' any flatMap implementation requires.
filter can not change the elements or their type, and can not add elements: useful restrictions for optimisations. Returning at most one element also avoids the 'sequence API'.

In many other approaches the lambda of `.flatMap` produces a flow, and this subtly interacts with fences/trickle down/lazy.
The lambda of our `.flatMap` returns a list, so each inner expansion is fenced.
There are two flatMap signatures:
`mut .flatMap[R:*](f: read F[E, read List[R]]): mut Flow[read/imm R]`

`mut .flatMapMut[R:*](f: read F[E, mut List[R]]): mut Flow[R]`

`.flatMap` takes a `read` list and hands its elements on as `read` or `imm`, as `++` does; the list of `.flatMapMut` is `mut`, since a `read` list hands its elements out as `read`.
The first is trivially sugar over the second
```
mut .flatMap[R:*](f: read F[E, read List[R]]): mut Flow[read/imm R]
  ->this.flatMapMut{e -> f#e .flow.list};
```
But the restricted signature of the first one allows for more parallelism.

The lambda builds its whole expansion before any element of it moves on.
Consider the following example:
```
Lists#(Lists#("aa","bbb"), Lists#("c","poison","dd"), Lists#("e"))
  .flow
  .flatMap{ss -> ss.flow
    .map{s -> s == "poison" ? {.then -> Error.msg`poison`; .else -> s}}.list}
  .limit 3
  .map{s -> s.size}
  .list
```
This example throws: generating each list runs its whole nested flow, and the one of the second element meets `"poison"`.
The same chain with `.limit 2` returns `Lists#(2, 3)`, since the second element is never pulled.

In most other approaches the flatMap lambda returns a flow, so one can write
```
Lists#(Lists#("aa","bbb"), Lists#("c","poison","dd"), Lists#("e"))
  .flow
  .flatMap{ss -> ss.flow
    .map{s -> s == "poison" ? {.then -> Error.msg`poison`; .else -> s}}}
  .limit 3
  .map{s -> s.size}
  .list
```
That example succeeds, returning `Lists#(2, 3, 1)`: `.limit 3` stops the inner `map` before it reaches `"poison"`.

In our approach, any inner flow in the lambda is normal code, run in parallel when possible under the same rules as any other code.

**Intermediate mut objects:**\
Consider
```
numbers.flow
  .map{n->Persons#n}
  .filter{..}.list
```
with `Persons#` creating `mut Person` objects.
The most traditional map-reduce fork-join parallelisation applies to such code.
```
numbers.flow
  .map{n->Persons#n}
  .flatMapMut{p->Lists#(p,p)}
  .filter{..}.list
```
Crucially, this variant can not be parallelised in the same way:
it would put the same `p` in two parallel tasks.
This is why there are two signatures:
```
numbers.flow
  .map{n->Persons#n}
  .flatMap{p->..}//the elements coming out of it are read or imm, never mut
  .filter{..}.list
```
`.flatMapMut` has to stop fork-join parallelisation at that stage: the elements it produces from the same input may share `mut` objects (see **Duplicated `mut` elements**).
`.flatMap` can keep the parallelisation going forward.

`.flatMapMut` is expressible with the actors, using the `forEach` that hands a hygienic value to every call (see **forEach**):

```
.flatMapMut f -> this.actorMut[Void, R](Void, {down, _, e ->
  Block#(f#e .seqFlow.forEach(down, {d, x -> d.push#(d, x)}), Continue#)});
```


