### ++

`mut ++ (b: read List[E]): mut Flow[read/imm E]`

`++` is a fence: every element of the receiver goes through the stages before the `++` before any element goes past it, and the elements of `b` follow those of the receiver.
It is useful both in the par modes and to start parallelisation after the fact.
After the `++` every element is `read` or `imm` and the stages before it have finished, so nothing the later stages can reach is still changing: even if the receiver was not a parallel flow, the stages after the `++` can run data parallel on all the elements, of the receiver and of `b`.
This starts parallel execution after a sequential stage:
`persons.seqFlow.map{p->p.computeDNA}++{}.map{..}.list`
This is arguably not the most readable code, but appending the empty list gives type safe knowledge that the second map can be data parallel.

It is the fence, not the types alone, that makes this sound. Two persons of `persons` can share one `mut Address`, and in
```
persons.seqFlow.map{p -> Block#(p.moveTo(p.name), p)} ++ {} .map{q -> q.address.street}.list
```
trickling without the fence would read the street of the first person before the second person moves, while a parallel run could read it after. With the fence, both streets are read after every move, in every mode.



### size, get, getOpt

`size` is just sugar for `.list.size`, even where it could be faster, because
those optimisations are really brittle.
On many other systems we would have
```
es.map.size//fast
es.map.filter.size//slow
```

`get` and `getOpt` are sugar over `.list` in the same way:
```
.get -> this.list |> {xs -> xs.size == 1 ? {
  .then -> xs.getFirst;
  .else -> Error.msg(`Flow.get: the flow has `+(xs.size)+` elements, but .get needs exactly one`)}};
.getOpt -> this.list |> {xs -> xs.size <= 1 ? {
  .then -> xs.first;
  .else -> Error.msg(`Flow.getOpt: the flow has `+(xs.size)+` elements, but .getOpt needs at most one`)}};
```
The size is checked once every element has passed the whole chain, so
```
Lists#(1,2,3).flow.map{x -> x == 3 ? {.then -> Error.msg`three`; .else -> x}}.get
```
raises `three`, not the error about the size.
Deciding at the second element would make them short-circuit terminals, and every element computed past the second would be positive speculation, which is not safe over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`.
As consuming terminals they are parallelised exactly like `.list`.

### mapping

`l.flow.map f .mapping(by, kem)`

Let `put` be what the terminal does with one element: `put#(x)` applies `kem.key` and `kem.elem`
to `x`, uses `by` to place the key among the keys already present, and throws when the key is
already present. Which calls `put` makes is left under-specified, as under `.sort`.
If there is a smallest index `j` such that `put#(f#ej)` throws or does not terminate,
the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate.
Otherwise this flow returns the map holding one entry per element, in index order.

Two elements carrying the same key make `put` throw at the later of the two indices, so the flow
raises rather than keeping one value and dropping the other; in other flow/stream libraries the same chain may silently keep either value.

**Parallelisation options:**

Data parallel:

`put` has two halves. Applying `kem.key` and `kem.elem` to `x` depends only on `x`, so it is elementwise and joins `map f` in one parallel region: each task turns its chunk into key and element pairs.
Placing the keys can not be split: it is where a repeated key is found, and the calls it makes to `by` must be the same in every mode, so they are the calls of placing the keys one at a time, in index order, into a single map. Building one map per chunk and merging them would call `by` on different pairs, which is observable when `by` throws or does not terminate.
The placement then runs sequentially, taking the pairs in index order as the tasks complete them. An `Info` raised by `f`, `kem.key` or `kem.elem` at index `j` is held until the placement reaches `j`: if the placement finds a repeated key at an earlier index, that one is raised instead, and the held `Info` is negative speculation that never surfaces.

Nodes:

It can work as a two actor system: one worker computes `map f`, `kem.key` and `kem.elem`, and one places the pairs into the map being built. Messages naturally arrive in order.
The two overlap in time: the placing worker places element `j` while the first computes element `j+1`. The same holds when a serial stage, such as an actor, comes before `.mapping`: the terminal's own per-element work overlaps the later elements of that stage.
As usual, `Yield#` is needed to terminate looping speculative execution after an error is raised.

### forEach

A Java stream forEach prevents most parallelism:
forEach needs a mut lambda to make any sense, and that makes code like
```
v:Var[Nat]
Lists#(0,..,64).flow
  .map{_ -> v.get}
  .forEach{x -> Block#(acc.add x, v.set(v.get + 1))}
```
to be parallelism sensitive.
In Fearless `.forEach` is a fence (see **Flow abstraction**), so that example behaves the same in every mode (see **example with forEach**).

In plenty of situations a fence does NOT need materialisation:
it is just a semantic guarantee that can be optimised away.
Consider
```
Lists#(0,..,64).flow
  .map{..}//could capture v as before
  .forEach{..}
```
This needs materialisation if v is captured as 'read'.
The `onlyImmCapture` flag (see **Execution modes**) decides whether to
trigger the fence, with no need for `.immMap` or other methods taking `imm F` instead of `read F`.
With the flag, nothing forEach can mutate is visible to .map, so there is no need to enforce the fence.
This holds even if some calls in `.map{..}` throw:
any change .forEach may make is garbage when that error is captured.
This avoids materialising large ranges, as in
`0~~10000000 .flow.map{..}.forEach{..}`

`forEach` also has a version that hands a hygienic value to every call, as `Block.loop(a, body)` does:
```
mut .forEach[A](a: mutH A, f: mut ForEachBody[A,E]): Void;
ForEachBody[A,E:*]: { mut #(a: mutH A, e: E): Void }
```
With it an `actorMut` lambda can push every element of a list on its sink, as the desugaring of `.flatMapMut` does.

### let
Let is the most unusual operation: it declares a local variable in the middle of a flow.

```
es.flow.map{i -> Persons#(..)}
  .let stuff = {listUpToNow -> ..}
  .seqFlow
  .map{..}
  .list
```

`let` is a terminal, not a primitive:

```
.let(x, cont) -> this.list |>{xs->cont#(x#xs, xs)};
```
It consumes the flow into a list, hands that list to `x`, and hands
`cont` both the result of `x` and the same list.
The elements are needed twice and a flow consumes once, so they are materialised here.
Handing on the list rather than a flow makes that visible and allows parallelism:
the user calls `.flow` or `.seqFlow` on it.
