## Examples of combining operations

Assuming: `Boom#` throwing a deterministic error, `l:List[Nat]` containing `0` to `7`, `ErrIf#(b: Bool, x: Nat): Nat` raising a deterministic error when `b` holds and returning `x` otherwise, `f` and `g` of type `read F[Nat,Nat]`, `P` and `Q` of type `read F[Nat,Bool]`, `by` the natural order on the elements.//TODO: reduce and simplify this somehow. Some of those can be moved to be local. Like.. can we just write Lists#(0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7) in the relevant examples?

**example with size**\
`l.flow.map{x -> Boom#}.size`

This code throws. In other flow/stream libraries this may return the size without executing the lambda, since the size is known.

**example with a short-circuit terminal**\
`l.flow.map{x -> ErrIf#(x == 5, x)}.first`

This code returns the first element; the error carried by element 5 is never raised. Any execution strategy that evaluates element 5 has to either stop the error propagation or throw a non deterministic error.\
In other flow/stream libraries a parallel execution may non deterministically propagate that error out.

**example with filter, under a short-circuit terminal**\
`l.flow.filter{x -> P#x}.any{x -> Q#x}`

This flow returns `True` if there is an index `i` such that:

- `P#ei && {Q#ei}` returns `True` (that is, it does not loop and does not throw an exception)
- forall `j<i`, `P#ej && {Q#ej}` returns `False`

It returns `False` if `P#ej && {Q#ej}` returns `False` for every index `j`.

If neither case applies, there is a smallest index `i` such that `P#ei && {Q#ei}` throws or does not terminate, and the whole flow expression propagates the result of that expression: the same `Info`, or non-termination.

This example shows clearly what "trickle the elements through the stages one at a time" means.

**example with a fence**\
```
l.flow.map f .sort by .filter {::>3}.first
```
If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate.\
Otherwise, if any call of `by.cmp(...)` used in the sorting process throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate.\
Otherwise, let `v0,...,vn` be the `f#ej` in `by` order, ties ordered by smallest index, and let `k` be the smallest position such that `{::>3}` applied to `vk` throws, does not terminate, or returns `True`.\
When no such `k` exists, this flow returns the empty `Opt`. When `{::>3}` applied to `vk` throws, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info`. When `{::>3}` applied to `vk` does not terminate, neither does the whole flow expression.\
Otherwise this flow returns `Opts#(vk)`.

Which calls to `by.cmp(...)` the sorting process makes, and in which order, is intentionally under-specified; the sorting process is under the control of the terminal operation. So `.sort by .first` could be optimised to just find the minimum element with the lowest index.

**Parallelisation options:**

Data parallel:

The stage computing `map f` can be parallelised. The sorting can not start until all the `f#ej` are computed. Computation of the stages before and after the fence does not overlap.

After the fence the sorted sequence is finished and of known size, so the filter stage can be parallelised again. The terminal `.first` controls this, by speculatively executing chunks of elements in `.sort` order.

To preserve the observable behaviour, the results of any `.filter` call after the first one not returning `False` are discarded. That call could return `True`, throw an exception or loop. If it loops, it remains uncertain which call is the first, so the other filter results are kept on hold indefinitely.\
Speculative executions that can no longer impact the sequential result can be terminated only if they use `Yield#` correctly.

Nodes:
```
l.flow.map f .sort by .filter {::>3}.first
```
The map actor produces elements; the sort actor can use an algorithm like insertion sort to place them as they arrive one by one,
and must finish sorting before sending anything downstream.
Filter and First can be merged into a single
FilterFirst actor that sends a termination signal to the other actors when it finds the first valid element.

It is tempting to instead merge sort and filter, to sort fewer elements faster, but this gives an observably different sorting semantic if `by` does not respect its conventional contract. Our formal properties must hold on all code, not just on code respecting logical but unenforced contracts.
This may mean that 'sometimes use insertion sort' is not allowed either.

**example with distinct, on a repeated element**\
```
Lists#(1, 1, 2).flow.map f
  .distinct by .filter {::>3}.list
```
If there is a smallest index `i` such that `f#ei |> {e -> ...}` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate. The body of that pipe `|>` places `e` among the values already kept and, when `e` is kept, applies `{::>3}` to `e`; the body makes calls like `by.hash(e)` and `by.cmp(e, f#ej)` with `j < i`, and which of them it makes is left under-specified, as under `.sort`.\
Otherwise this flow returns, in index order, those of the kept values greater than `3`, where the kept values are `f#1` and `f#2` when `by` ranks `f#1` and `f#2` apart, and `f#1` alone when `by` ranks them together.

**Parallelisation options:**

Data parallel:

`map f` runs in chunks, and so can computing the key and hash of each result, which depend only on that element. Placing the keys can not be split: it is where a repeated key is found, so it runs sequentially, taking the results in source order as the tasks complete them. The kept elements are handed on in chunks to `filter` tasks as soon as they are kept, without waiting for the `map` or `distinct` stage to complete, and `.list` joins the chunks in order.
An `Info` raised at index `i`, by `f`, by `by` or by the filter, is raised only if nothing raises at an earlier index; otherwise it is negative speculation that never surfaces.

Nodes:

A `map f` node, a `distinct` node and a `filter` node, the last also building the list. Each node receives the elements in source order, so the `distinct` node needs no reordering: it keeps the kept keys as local mutable data, a hash set shared with no one, and passes each kept element on at once. The `map f` node can also compute the keys, leaving the `distinct` node only the placing.

**example with limit**\
`l.flow.map f .limit 3 .map g .list`

If there is a smallest index `j` among `0`, `1` and `2` so that the expression `g#(f#ej)` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate. Otherwise this flow returns `Lists#(g#(f#e0), g#(f#e1), g#(f#e2))`.

The result does not depend on `f#ej` for any `j` above `2`, so an error raised by `f#e5` or `g#(f#e5)` is never reported.\
In other flow/stream libraries a parallel execution may non deterministically propagate that error out.

**example with actorMut**\
```
l.flow
  .map f
  .actorMut[mut Var[Nat], Nat](Vars#[Nat]0, {down, s, e ->
    Block#(s.set(s.get + e), down.push#(down, s.get), Continue#)})
  .map g .list
```

Let `h` be the actor lambda, `s` the state built by `Vars#[Nat]0`, `down` the sink that applies `g` to each value handed to it, and `sj` the sum of `f#e0` up to `f#ej`. If there is a smallest index `j` such that `h#(down, s, f#ej)` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate.\
Otherwise this flow returns the list of `g#s0` to `g#s7`.

The stages computing `map f` and `map g` can be parallelised. The actor cannot, because the call for one element observes what the call for an earlier element left in the state. Multiple actor stages could run pipeline parallel with each other.

The three regions still overlap in time: the actor takes what `map f` has finished (in source order), and `map g` takes what the actor has emitted. No fence is needed, since the actor emits one element at a time.

One cost remains: the actor needs the elements in index order, so the results of `map f` are put back in order before the actor sees them; parallelising only chunks of limited size bounds the reordering buffer.
//TODO: again the text above would need to be reorganized to have a data parallel and a node section. Given this is actorMut, probably the node section would have advantages.

**example with let**\
```
l.flow
  .map f
  .let[Nat, mut List[Nat]] total = {xs -> xs.flow.sum 0}
  .flow
  .map{n -> n + total}
  .list
```

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate.\
Otherwise this flow returns the list holding `f#ej + total` at each index `j`, where `total` is the sum of `f#ej` over every index `j`.

The result of every `f#ej` is needed twice, so `let` materialises the flow up to that point as a list.

**example with forEach**\
`l.flow.map{_ -> v.get} .forEach{x -> Block#(acc.add x, v.set(v.get + 1))}`\
with `v` a fresh `mut Var[Nat]` holding `0`, and `acc` a fresh `mut EList[Nat]`.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `v.get |> {x -> Block#(acc.add x, v.set(v.get + 1))}` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate.\
Otherwise this flow returns `Void`, and afterwards `acc` holds eight times `0` and `v` holds `8`.

The `map` lambda reads `v` and the terminal lambda writes `v`. Crucially `.forEach` is a fence operation.

In many other flow/stream approaches forEach is not a fence, and `acc` may end up holding `0` to `7` instead. Fearless flows need the fence to allow parallelism: by its nature, `.forEach` performs side effects on mut captured values.

With the fence the stage can be parallelised: every `v.get` reads the same `0`, and no stage call reads what the terminal writes, since the terminal runs after the fence and forEach is the only terminal that can mutate existing data.

**example with filter, over ParRepr**\
```
rs.flow(ParRepr)
  .filter{r -> r.mutate{
    c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}
    > 3}
  .size
```
If any two `Repr`s (or `IsoPod`s in an equivalent example) are the same object, a non deterministic exception is thrown.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `rj.mutate{c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate. Thanks to strong exception safety we will never be able to observe how many elements in the flow/list have been mutated.\
Otherwise this flow returns the number of indices `j` where `rj` holds more than `3` after being increased, and afterwards every `rj` in `rs` holds one more than `rj` held before the flow.

Every element is increased, the rejected elements included, and `rs` still holds every element, so the increases are readable once the flow has returned.

The filter can be parallelised while `size` is the terminal.

Consider the same example but with a short-circuit terminal like `.any` in place of `size`
```
rs.flow(ParRepr)
  .filter{r -> r.mutate{
    c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}
    > 3}
  .any {r->r.look{..}}
```
In many other approaches the parallelisation becomes (positive) speculative, and the increases on elements past the decision survive the flow.
We can not accept this.
This is exactly code screaming 'parallel' that is only safe to execute sequentially,
so it throws a non deterministic exception, even when the optimiser would 'want' to run it sequentially.

The only sound option is to insert a fence before
any operation that would need (positive) speculative execution to run in parallel:
```
rs.flow(ParRepr)
  .filter{r -> r.mutate{
    c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}
    > 3}
  .sort {..}
  .any {r->r.look{..}}
```
Overall, parallelising `Repr`s and `IsoPod`s is about
- running an identity check
- checking the `onlyImmCapture` flag on the lambdas of the flow
- avoiding unfenced (positive) speculative execution and running fenced (positive) speculative execution sequentially
- behaving exactly as if a par flow produced a mut out of the first mapping: those muts are isolated from each other and from what the lambdas captured, so they can not interact until a `flatMapMut` or an `actorMut` duplicates them (see **Duplicated `mut` elements**). Depending on the stages, data parallel or nodes could be more appropriate.


